## §2 Execution phases — `POD_execute_combat_move`

Single generic effect that executes any combat move by reading
attributes off its story_cycle. Lives in
`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`. Called from:

- `pod_combat_execute_move` SGUI (mid-combat PC button)
- `pod_combat_ai_select_and_execute_move_effect` (AI turn)

**Caller scope:** combat host (the PC). The workspace alias pair
(`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*`) is loaded against
the acting character's slot before this fires; "actor" = whoever is
taking the action this turn (PC OR any AI combatant), "target" = the
current single-target (rotates per AoE iteration / AI swap). The
workspace is assumed loaded by the caller.

Phases run top-to-bottom. The cross-phase invariants below are
load-bearing — reordering will silently break behavior.

| § | Phase | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| §0 | Capture combat host scope | saved as `scope:combat_host` so AoE iteration bodies can re-enter host scope without their own root-save dance |
| §1 | AP spend | must precede §6 (committed move) |
| §2 | Result-flag reset + log metadata | must precede every §3-§15 write |
| §3 | Energy cost | runs on actor scope (not host) |
| §4 | CK3 resource costs | PC only (player/0) |
| §5 | Target stance bypass | strips workspace target stances (`bypass_dodge` / `bypass_stealth` / `remove_guard`) before §6 reads |
| §5b | AoE debuff pre-broadcast | strips slot stances; must precede §6 so per-target damage iteration sees the post-strip state |
| §6 | HP damage dispatch | reads §5 / §5b stance state. Writes `pod_combat_last_outcome` (=`flag:attack`) and `pod_combat_last_attack_result` on the host (consumed by the rider gates §7 / §8 / §9) |
| §7 | Morale damage | independent of §6 in pipeline, but rider-gated by `morale_damage_condition`. Default = always; set to `on_hp_damage` / `on_contact` / `on_miss` to make the morale payload conditional |
| §8 | Self-damage | rider-gated by `self_damage_condition`. Default = always (current reckless-charge behavior). No snapshot/restore needed under the B1 scope-export contract — the bypass-guard damage call returns via scope values and never touches `pod_combat_last_*` host vars, so §6's writes survive untouched |
| §9 | HP heal | rider-gated by `heal_condition`. Default = always; legacy drain moves emit `heal_condition = on_hp_damage` at codegen so they still gate on a landed hit. MUST run AFTER §6 for the rider gate to read the right outcome |
| §10 | Morale heal | currently always-fire (no rider condition wired). Symmetric extension is one column away if a future move needs it |
| §11 | Tier buffs/debuffs | applied to actor / target. Should precede §12 so a "buff yourself then guard" composes as expected (stat-then-stance) |
| §12 | Stance flags (`set_guard` / `dodge` / `stealth`) | applied to actor AFTER damage, so a "set guard then attack" attacks first then guards (intuitive ordering for the end-of-turn defensive stance) |
| §12b | AoE buff broadcast | extends §11/§12 to allies on `team_ally` moves; runs after actor's own buffs are written |
| §13 | Retaliation setup | actor-side; the `retaliate_*` workspace slots written here arm reactions for the NEXT turn's §15 to consume |
| §13b | DoT application | seeds `slot_dot_amount/rounds/kind/aggravated` on the target's slot (single-target; an AoE DoT would fold into the §5b/AoE phase). Kind is seeded as LITERAL flags only (default duel, per-source override, morale wins); re-apply refreshes. Pushes the per-type "is afflicted" log row. Ticks fire at round end — see **DoT primitive** in §7 |
| §13c | Mind control application | dispatched on the `mind_control` move flag: marks the target slot `slot_controlled` (one stolen turn). Gates: players immune; `POD_has_strong_mental_defenses_trigger` blocks unless the caster passes `can_bypass_mental_defenses_trigger`. Blocked casts post a visible resist row. Single-target only |
| §14 | Parametric flags (AP gain/drain, `initiative_debuff`) | order-independent. Drain writes are take-max so a later small drain can never shrink a pending stun (`ap_drain_6`) |
| §14b | Summon a combatant | dispatched on the `summons_combatant` flag -> `pod_combat_summon_combatant_glue_effect = { MOVE_STORY }`, which creates a character, applies the move's `summon_recipe` (`pod_combat_apply_summon_recipe_effect`), and seats it on the caster's team mid-fight via `pod_combat_add_ally_midfight_effect` / `_add_enemy_midfight_effect`. Runs AFTER the damage/AoE phases (a same-cast AoE can't hit the new unit) and before §15; the summon sits out the current round and joins initiative next round-start (`slot_has_acted = 1`). Every mid-fight seat posts a named "joins the fray" log row. See **Mid-combat add** under §3 |
| §15 | Reaction phase | fires reactions armed by the PREVIOUS turn's §13. Each reaction is gated by a result-code partition trigger (`pod_combat_result_code_indicates_*`) that reads §6's outcome on this scope. Today: retaliate-on-contact (hp damage / guard blocked / soaked all qualify — thorns semantics) and retaliate-on-evasion (miss / dodge / stealth all qualify — riposte semantics). See **Reaction system** below |
| §16 | Push log | reads ALL prior flags + amount fields. MUST run last |

### Anti-invariants — orderings that MUST NOT happen

- §6 before §5/§5b — damage hits unstripped stances.
- §7/§8/§9 before §6 — rider gates read `pod_combat_last_outcome`
  and `pod_combat_last_attack_result`, both set by §6. Reordering
  breaks `on_hp_damage` / `on_contact` / `on_miss` conditions.
- §15 before §6 — retaliation reads stale state from prior move.
- §16 anywhere but last — log captures partial result, missing the
  rider amounts the later phases populated.

### Adding a new phase

Pick a numeric position, document its dependencies in this section,
and update the result-flag reset block (§2) and the cleanup block in
`pod_combat_cleanup_effect` if it produces a new flag.

### Turn loop — how turns are sequenced

`POD_execute_combat_move` above resolves ONE action. The turn loop
(`pod_combat_group_effects.txt`) decides *whose* action runs next and
when control returns to the GUI. It is built from one atomic step plus
two drain wrappers, so the same pointer-advance happens in every
context (combat start, mid-round, round boundary).

**The atomic step — `pod_combat_advance_one_ai_turn_effect`.**
`find_next`-first: select the next actor by initiative, then execute
exactly one AI turn. Shape is `pod_combat_find_next_actor_effect`
followed by a guarded `pod_combat_execute_ai_turn_effect`. The execute
guard skips the call when the pointer is cleared, combat is over, OR
the selected actor is **player/0** — the load-bearing case is the
pre-PC walk, where the PC is still unacted so `find_next` can
legitimately land on it; a bare execute would then run the PC's turn
as if it were AI. (In the post-PC main loop the PC is already acted,
so `find_next` never returns player/0 and the guard never fires.) A
player-team ally AI at position > 0 is a genuine AI step and still
executes — the guard is position-0-only.

**The two drains, both bodied by the atomic step:**

- `pod_combat_run_remaining_ai_turns_effect` — the post-PC auto
  drain. `while` over "any alive + not-acted slot, combat not over",
  one atomic step per iteration. Terminates because each step marks
  one slot acted.
- `pod_combat_run_pre_pc_ai_turns_effect` — the combat-start /
  round-start walk. One prime `find_next` (so the `while` limit can
  read `current_actor` on the first pass — callers therefore no
  longer pre-call `find_next`), then `while` over the same alive +
  not-acted set BUT additionally stopping the moment the pointer
  reaches player/0. On a clean exit it leaves `current_actor` on the
  PC and runs `pod_combat_setup_pc_turn_effect` inline.

**Entry points — End Turn vs Advance.** Two SGUIs
(`pod_combat_skill_guis.txt`) drive the loop from the GUI, and the
split is the whole point of the decomposition:

- **End Turn** (`pod_combat_end_turn` → `pod_combat_process_group_turns_effect`).
  Reachable ONLY when the PC is the current actor. Carries the
  **once-per-PC-turn prologue** — mark player/0 acted + save the PC
  workspace — exactly once. This is the only effect that carries that
  prologue.
- **Advance** (`pod_combat_advance_turn`). The dedicated mid-step
  entry point, **prologue-free** by design: it never marks the PC
  acted and never saves the PC workspace, so it is safe to fire
  repeatedly while an AI turn is pending. It resolves the *currently
  pending* AI actor that `find_next` already primed.

`pod_combat_setup_pc_turn_effect` is the hand-back point reached from
all three PC-first sites (finalize, end-round, the pre-PC walk): it
restores the PC target, loads the workspace, recalcs the PC hit
chance, and resets / applies AP for the player's turn. The
round-boundary transition lives in `pod_combat_group_end_round_effect`
(round increment, log row, DoT tick, reinforcement seating,
initiative recalc, round-tier revert, then a PC-first-vs-AI-first
branch). The combat-start equivalent is the round-1 branch at the
tail of `POD_combat_finalize_duel_effect`. Both branches are
mode-aware — see *Step mode* in §7 for how STEP vs AUTO routes
through these same effects.

