## §4 Result codes

Three-axis result model held on the combat host across three
orthogonal vars; entry-side mirrors are captured by `push_log` onto
the log story (`entry.var:outcome` / `attack_result` / `retal_fired`).
Helpers along the damage / heal / morale pipelines write to the
host vars during move execution; `push_log` reads them, captures
them onto the entry, and the log dispatcher (`PODCombatLogResultText`)
plus the retal rider customizable_loc render the row.

**The three axes:**

| Host var | Entry mirror | Values | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `pod_combat_last_outcome` | `entry.var:outcome` | `flag:attack \| heal \| morale_damage \| morale_healed \| utility` | Top-level outcome flag — "what kind of action was this row". Defaults to `flag:utility` if no helper wrote a value. |
| `pod_combat_last_attack_result` | `entry.var:attack_result` | `flag:hp_damage \| soaked \| missed \| dodged \| stealthed \| guard_blocked` | Inner attack resolution. ONLY meaningful when `outcome=flag:attack`; readers gate on the outcome axis first. |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` | `entry.var:retal_fired` | `1` (present) or absent | Overlay flag — set unconditionally by the retaliation helper whenever a counter-strike resolved. Independent of outcome and attack_result. |

`flag:stealthed` is distinct from `flag:dodged` so the loc line
links to the Stealth concept (not Dodge); both mechanically auto-miss.

**Sentinel handling:** §2 of `POD_execute_combat_move` removes all
three host vars (via `pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect`) so
absence is the "no result yet" sentinel for the new move's pipeline.
Helpers write the matching axis vars as the move resolves. `push_log`
defaults to `flag:utility` for `entry.var:outcome` when the host var
is still unset at log-push time; `attack_result` and `retal_fired`
are simply not captured when their host vars are unset.

**Priority semantics:** The damage pipeline writes the outcome /
attack_result pair in lockstep via
`pod_combat_set_attack_result_effect` (outcome=flag:attack +
attack_result=$CODE$). Secondary outcomes (morale damage, HP heal,
morale heal) use `pod_combat_set_outcome_if_unset_effect` so they
only become the primary outcome when no earlier writer has fired —
a damage+heal move keeps outcome=attack as primary; the heal amount
rides along in `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` and the heal rider
customizable_loc emits a side line.

Retaliation is an INDEPENDENT overlay (not a transform of the
outcome flag). `pod_combat_set_retal_fired_effect` sets
`pod_combat_last_retal_fired = 1` regardless of the main attack's
outcome — so a missed main attack with a landed retal preserves
the missed outcome AND signals the counter-strike via the
PODCombatLogRetalRider customizable_loc. (The previous single-flag
model overwrote the main attack's outcome with `hp_damage_retal` /
`soaked_retal` composite flags, which silently lost the main
attack's actual result when retaliation overwrote it.)

**Self-damage.** §8 of `POD_execute_combat_move` discards the
scope exports from the bypass-guard damage call entirely — the
main attack's outcome / attack_result / damage vars survive the
self-damage call untouched because `pod_combat_route_to_pod_damage`
now uses a scope-export-only return contract (see B1 refactor) and
nothing host-side gets clobbered. No snapshot/restore needed.

**Result-code partition triggers** (in
`pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt`) compose against the
outcome + attack_result axes to express the rider / reaction gates.
Each gates on `outcome = flag:attack` first to ensure attack_result
is meaningful before checking specific flag values:

- `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_contact_trigger` —
  outcome=attack + attack_result ∈ (hp_damage, guard_blocked, soaked).
  HP damage / blocked / soaked.
- `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_miss_trigger` —
  outcome=attack + attack_result ∈ (missed, dodged, stealthed).
- `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_hp_damage_trigger` —
  outcome=attack + attack_result=hp_damage (a wound went through).

A parametric trigger
`pod_combat_result_indicates_no_damage_dealt_trigger = { RESULT_VAR = X }`
reads the attack_result enum off either `pod_combat_last_attack_result`
(host scope, RESULT_VAR=pod_combat_last_attack_result) or
`entry.var:attack_result` (log entry scope, RESULT_VAR=attack_result),
used by the GUI's damage-source icon suppression on no-damage rows.

### AoE log shape (Phase C)

AoE moves (`targeting = team_enemy / team_ally`) push **one log
row per target** as the iteration progresses, instead of leaving
§16 to capture only the last-target state and rescuing it with a
dispatcher-side intent fallback. The four AoE iterators
(`pod_combat_apply_team_enemy_hp_damage` /
`_morale_damage` / `pod_combat_apply_team_ally_hp_heal` /
`_morale_heal`) each call `pod_combat_push_log_effect` inside the
loop after the per-target effect resolves, capturing the per-target
outcome / attack_result / damage / target onto the new log
entry. Per-target state is cleared between iterations via
`pod_combat_clear_per_target_state_effect` (which preserves the
`pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows` tripwire flag — distinct from
the full `pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect` used at §2 /
push_log post-capture / cleanup).

Each per-target row records its target from the workspace's
`pod_combat_target_char` (the iterator loads each target into the
workspace before its push), so there is no separate target_side
capture. The host's `pod_combat_selected_target_team` /
`pod_combat_selected_target_position` stay on the move's primary target
throughout the AoE iteration so it can be restored cleanly by the
post-loop `pod_combat_load_workspace_effect`.

§16's wrap-up `push_log` is gated: when the AoE flag is set,
§16 only fires if actor-side state remains (self-heal amount,
self-morale-heal amount, or retal_fired overlay) — otherwise the
per-target rows are the whole log entry for the move, and no
summary row is pushed.

The single-flag-era AoE intercept (3 dispatcher branches +
3 loc keys + `pod_combat_log_entry_attack_failed_trigger`) was
dropped wholesale alongside the per-target shape — those existed
to rescue the misleading last-target capture under the old single-
row contract. The per-target contract makes them obsolete.

### Row rendering — head + segments

A move row's result column is a **head plus an ordered list of
segments**, composed left-to-right by the GUI hbox.

**The head** is `PODCombatLogResultText` (customizable_localization),
consuming two saved scopes — `entry` (the log entry story: outcome /
attack_result / retal_fired axes + amount fields) and `move` (the
executing move story). Branches compose on the outcome axis first;
HP-track outcomes split into `_aggravated` variants off
`scope:move.var:damage_type` / `heal_type`. The damage-source mark
renders beside the head ONLY for direct damage (`move.hp_damage` AND
`move.damage_source` both set, suppressed on no-damage outcomes) — a
source that types a secondary effect is marked by that effect's own
segment.

**Segments** each consist of exactly: a visible SV
(`pod_combat_log_seg_<x>_visible_value`, entry scope, returns 1 iff
the renderer emits content), a renderer (`PODCombatLogSeg<X>`
customizable_loc reading ONLY its own fields, with a defensive
no-move first branch because the GUI evaluates raw_text even on
invisible widgets), and a separator + content widget pair built from
the `PODSegVisible` / `PODSegSepVisible` / `PODSegText` data-binding
macros (`POD_combat_macros.txt`).

**Visibility and separators are baked at push time.** Entry / move
vars are immutable once a row is pushed, so `pod_combat_push_log_effect`
evaluates every segment condition once, in display order (move-var
checks, entry-amount checks, outcome suppression, and the AoE burst
gate all live in the bake), stamping `seg_<x>_on` (segment renders)
and `seg_<x>_sep` (a separator precedes it) onto the entry. The
visible SVs and the `pod_combat_log_before_<x>_value` SVs are thin
`has_variable` reads of those vars, replacing the per-tick linked
accumulator chain. Rows that bypass the bake (system / DoT / mind /
daze) carry no seg vars and read all-invisible. A separator still
checks exactly two terms (own visible AND before), so appending a
segment never edits existing separators.

Segment order (outcome segments — entry-captured actuals — then
intent segments — move-story definitions):

| Segment | Source |
|---|---|
| Heal / Morale damage / Morale heal | outcome (amount fields, suppressed when outcome equals their primary) |
| Retal fired ("+ Counter ...") | outcome (`retal_fired` overlay) |
| Recoil (self damage) | outcome (`entry.self_damage`, lifted at §8) |
| Hit tier, Soak tier | intent (tier vars; one segment per stat) |
| Guard / Dodge / Stealth / Guard strip | intent (stance flags) |
| Retal armed ("→ Retaliate" + own type mark, morale > aggravated > source) | intent |
| AP gain, AP drain ("Stuns" at >= base AP), Initiative debuff | intent |

Intent segments gate on `aoe_burst_index` unset-or-0 so move-level
effects render once per AoE burst; outcome segments render on
whichever per-target row captured their amount.

The retal overlay (`entry.var:retal_fired`) remains independent of
the main outcome — every main outcome can carry the counter line
without composite `_retal` flags.

